# Netlify security headers.
#
# This file lives in the published folder rather than in netlify.toml on
# purpose. A netlify.toml "publish" setting is resolved relative to the site's
# Base directory, which is already set to "Frontend" in the dashboard — so
# publish = "Frontend" became "Frontend/Frontend" and broke the build. A
# _headers file is read from the deploy directory itself, so it works whatever
# the base and publish settings are.
#
# Netlify already sends Strict-Transport-Security; these are the ones it does
# not set by default.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=()

# A full CSP is deliberately not set: every page carries inline <style> and
# inline <script>, so any workable script-src/style-src policy would need
# 'unsafe-inline' and would buy very little.

/*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
